%description:
Field-level check of the deserializers against an independent decoder.

Both round-trip tests reproduce the bytes of a frame, which is exactly why neither can
see a serializer that reads and writes a field in the same wrong place: the bytes come
back identical whatever the fields mean. A swap of two same-width neighbours, a value
read one field too early, a constant in the wrong place -- all of that survives a
round-trip untouched. It was proved on the case: swapping the receiver and transmitter
addresses of the 802.11 serializer in both directions leaves every round-trip green and
fails this test on both fields.

What catches it is a second opinion about the same bytes. Every expectation here comes
from a `.fields` file next to the capture, written by tests/serializer/tools/mkfieldrefs.py from
what tshark reads. The tool holds the mapping between tshark's field names and INET's, so
this test needs none of its own and never runs tshark; the file it commits is already in
INET's vocabulary, which keeps a Wireshark field rename inside the tool rather than in the
repository.

What the reference is worth depends on the mapping being right, and that is what its last
column is for: it names the tshark field every value came from, so a reviewer can check
the pairing. When a capture changes, regenerate the file and read the diff -- that diff is
the review.

Two rules for what is worth having in a reference:

  * neighbouring fields of the same width, where a swap is possible and invisible
    otherwise -- the three addresses of an 802.11 header, port pairs, the halves of a uuid;
  * fields with an independent reference: a configured parameter, an address that also
    appears a layer down, a counter that must increase.

It follows that this test only guards what the mapping covers -- the tool counts the
fields it had no mapping for, per frame, so what is missing stays visible. It is not a
replacement for the round-trips: it is the part they structurally cannot do.

The run says so itself, from the other side. After the result it prints how many fields of
the chunks the captures dissect an expectation names, and lists, per chunk class, the ones
none does. That list comes from INET's own class descriptors, so unlike the tool's count of
unmapped tshark fields it is exact, and it is the work list for extending the mapping: a
class that appears with most of its fields is one the corpus reaches but this test barely
looks at. Fields no decoder could have an opinion about are left out of it -- what the
simulation kernel and Chunk declare, the protocol-agnostic address and port views of
NetworkHeaderBase/TransportHeaderBase, which the concrete header also declares under its
own name, and the "...Mode" fields, which say how INET represents a value rather than what
went on the wire.

The numbers are printed, not asserted: the denominator moves whenever a message file gains
a field, and this test should not fail for that. What is asserted is the count of checks
made, so a lost expectation cannot pass unnoticed.

What is left on that list is accounted for one by one in REMAINING_GAPS.md, under
"What the field reference does not name": fields that are not on the wire at all, fields
that are a list inside a chunk and need a path the reference cannot yet write, fields no
frame in the corpus carries, and fields with nothing independent to compare against.

Set FIELDCHECK_VERBOSE in the environment to list the chunks of every frame the checks
look at, which is how a new expectation is placed.

%includes:
#include "SerializerTestLib.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;

%activity:

// keep the output quiet: results are logged at EV_WARN
cLog::logLevel = LOGLEVEL_WARN;

beginChunkFieldChecks();

// Every expectation comes from the .fields file next to the capture, written by
// tests/serializer/tools/mkfieldrefs.py from what tshark reads. The tool holds the mapping
// between tshark's field names and INET's, so this test needs none of its own -- and
// never runs tshark.
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/bgp.pcap", false);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/wlan-addba-delba.pcap", false);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/derived/sctp-www-clean.cap", false);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/generated/mld-query.pcap", true);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/generated/mrp-ring.pcap", true);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/generated/mrp-linkcheck.pcap", true);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/generated/mrp-mra.pcap", true);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/generated/mld-v2.pcap", true);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/generated/rtp-rtcp.pcap", true);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/generated/tsn-rtag.pcap", true);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/derived/mipv6-clean.pcap", false);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/rarp_request.pcap", false);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/qinq.pcap", false);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/mpls-ip.pcap", false);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/stp-tcn.pcap", false);
checkChunkFieldsFromReference("../../pcap/cfm.pcap", false);

// wlan-aggregation is left out on purpose: tshark decodes what is inside the A-MSDU
// subframes, INET leaves the subframe payload raw (see REMAINING_GAPS.md), so
// the reference would hold expectations about chunks this dissection does not produce.

reportChunkFieldChecks();

%contains: stdout
Field checks: 1825 made, 0 failed

%not-contains: stdout
FIELD-CHECK
